#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128815 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f8fa2b3-ccaa-5390-89fa-03e338cf8690" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Amplitude of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "e2e5002a-d3ae-5831-b405-41618960aee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-15609" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x) = -2\\sin\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3\\right) + 1$?" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)= A\\sin(Bx +C) + D\n$$\n\nthe amplitude is $\\mid A \\mid$.\n\nIn our case, we have that $A=-2$. Therefore, the amplitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128816 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1eeb42af-74ee-53d7-af9f-be171f77968f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7f6626c7-c3d3-5f4e-b2c7-1e9bf07e883c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15610" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=\\cos(2x)$." :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos(Bx +C) + D\n$$\n\nthe period is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{B}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=2$. Therefore, the period is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\left(2\\right)}=\\pi\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128817 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5696c2d5-4e35-5ed3-a4a2-6fe9b158e461" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "61b6821f-3ee5-5dd8-b8a3-ee1859645237" :question/math-academy-id "e-15611" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x) = -4\\cos(3x)-2$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\cos x$ is\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\cos x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\leq\\cos\\left(3x \\right) \\leq 1\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-4$ and then subtracting $2$, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}- 1 & \\le  \\cos   ( 3 x ) \\le  1 \\\\ 4 & \\ge  - 4 \\cos   ( 3 x ) \\ge  - 4 \\\\ 2 & \\ge  - 4 \\cos   ( 3 x ) - 2 \\ge  - 6 \\\\ - 6 & \\le  f ( x ) \\le  2.\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)\\in [-6,2]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128818 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "933b20c7-323f-5443-9cf4-97e87fa2c6fc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midline of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bfa446cd-c215-59c2-b84d-35d7897e9e34" :question/math-academy-id "e-15615" :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-6$ and a maximum value of $8$." :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half.\n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{y_{\\textrm{min}}+y_{\\textrm{max}}}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $y_{\\textrm{min}}$ and $y_{\\textrm{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y_{\\min} + y_{\\max}}{2} &= \\frac{-6 + 8}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "933b20c7-323f-5443-9cf4-97e87fa2c6fc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midline of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7123c4f-eb2f-5b18-b878-c2c2dc5dda4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126817} {:answer/id #uuid "39e58300-c394-50bb-9049-1f3aed0d5ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126818} {:answer/id #uuid "4264a0d0-ac3f-54e8-ac7b-beb85541ace9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value 